diff --git a/docs/emitter-design.md b/docs/emitter-design.md
index fa711c7..f6e5b8a 100644
--- a/docs/emitter-design.md
+++ b/docs/emitter-design.md
@@ -25,10 +25,19 @@ it).
 
 ## Register model
 
-Guest registers live in the context struct (`runtime/include/dream/runtime/sh4/ctx.h`), and the
-emitter accesses them as `c.r[n]`. No register caching in host locals in v1: it doubles the
-emitter's complexity for a gain the host compiler largely recovers on its own once the function is
-inlined into a few hundred lines of straight-line C++. Revisit after WP1.5 numbers exist.
+Guest registers live in the context struct (`runtime/include/dream/runtime/sh4/ctx.h`). v1 accessed
+them as `c.r[n]` everywhere, expecting the host compiler to keep them in registers. It does not:
+every guest store goes through `Memory`, which the compiler must assume can alias the context, so
+each register was reloaded after every store (SA-nx measured this on the Switch).
+
+SA-nx: with `EmitOptions::local_regs` (the default; `--no-local-regs` turns it off) the general
+registers a function uses are host locals (`gpr0` to `gpr15`), loaded from `c.r` after the entry
+poll and before the resume switch. They are written back (spilled) before every point where code
+outside the body can see or change `c.r`, and read again after the ones that return: direct and
+indirect calls, interrupt polls that deliver, `write_sr` (a bank switch swaps r0-r7), `trapa`,
+`unimplemented`, and before `rte`, every return and every tail call. A resumed body loads its locals
+from `c.r` like a plain entry. Floating point registers, T, PR, MACH/MACL and the cycle counter stay
+in the context. `tests/sh4/programs/p14_bank` pins the bank-switch case.
 
 The T bit is kept in `c.t` (0/1) and folded back into `c.sr` only where SR is observed (`STC SR`,
 `RTE`, `TRAPA`, interrupt delivery). Every emitted comparison writes `c.t` directly.
